
    BFS = Breadth First Search. Think of dropping a stone in water and watching ripples spread outward —
          you explore everything 1 step away first, then everything 2 steps away, then 3 steps away,
          expanding outward in rings.

    DFS = Depth First Search. Think of exploring a maze by picking one path and following it as far as you possibly can,
          until you hit a dead end — only then do you backtrack and try the next unexplored path.

    A visual example — let's use a simple grid of rooms
    A - B - C
    |       |
    D       E

    (A connects to B and D. B connects to A and C. C connects to B and E.)

    DFS starting from A: Go as deep as possible before backtracking.

    Visit A → go to B (deeper) → go to C (deeper) → go to E (deeper, dead end) → backtrack to C → nothing new →
    backtrack to B → nothing new → backtrack to A → go to D → dead end → done.
    Order visited: A, B, C, E, D

    BFS starting from A: Visit everything 1 step away first, then 2 steps away.

    Step 0: A
    Step 1 (1 step from A): B, D
    Step 2 (2 steps from A): C (from B)
    Step 3 (3 steps from A): E (from C)
    Order visited: A, B, D, C, E

    Notice the difference:
    DFS commits to one direction and goes deep before trying anything else.
    BFS spreads out evenly, layer by layer, like ripples.

    Why does this matter for a matrix?

    A matrix cell has neighbors too — up, down, left, right — just like rooms have doors.
    So "explore everything connected to this cell" is the exact same idea as exploring rooms.
    DFS on a matrix = pick a cell, go as far as you can in one direction (recursively visiting neighbors),
    backtrack when stuck.
    BFS on a matrix = visit all neighbors 1 step away first, then 2 steps away, etc. (using a queue).

    When do you use which?
    DFS — simpler to write (just recursion), good when you just need to find/mark/count connected things
    (like counting islands) — you don't care about the order or distance, just "are these connected?"
    BFS — use when you specifically need the shortest distance/path — because BFS visits things in order of distance
    (1 step away, then 2, then 3...), the first time it reaches a target, that's guaranteed to be the shortest path
    to it.

    Mapping rooms → matrix cells

    In a matrix, each cell (row, col) has up to 4 "doors" — its neighbors:

    up: (row-1, col)
    down: (row+1, col)
    left: (row, col-1)
    right: (row, col+1)

   DFS on a grid :
   1 1 0
   1 1 0
   0 0 1

   Step 1 — the "visited" tracker. Just like you wouldn't want to walk into room A again after already visiting it,
   we need to remember which cells we've already visited, so we don't visit them again (which could loop forever).
   boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];

   Step 2 — the DFS function itself. In plain English: "Visit this cell. Mark it visited. Then try to visit each of
   its 4 neighbors — but only if that neighbor is inside the grid, is a 1, and hasn't been visited yet."

   void dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c, boolean[][] visited) {
       // Step A: is this a valid cell to even look at?
       if (rows < 0 || rows >= grid.length || cols < 0 || cols >= grid[0].length) {
           return; // out of bounds — like trying to walk through a wall, just stop
       }
       if (visited[rows][cols] || grid[rows][cols] == 0) {
           return; // already visited — stop
       }

       // Step B: this cell is valid and unvisited — visit it
       visited[rows][cols] = true;
       System.out.println("Visited: (" + rows + ", " + cols + ")");

       // Step C: try visiting all 4 neighbors
       dfs(grid, rows - 1, cols, visited); // up
       dfs(grid, rows + 1, cols, visited); // down
       dfs(grid, rows, cols - 1, visited); // left
       dfs(grid, rows, cols + 1, visited); // right
   }

   BFS on a Grid :
   A queue works like a line at a shop: whoever gets in line first gets served first (First In, First Out — FIFO).
   This matters because BFS needs to explore things in the exact order it discovers them — cells 1 step away get
   explored before cells 2 steps away, because they were added to the queue first.

   Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();
   each int[] holds a {row, col} pair, representing one cell waiting to be explored.

   The three actions that happen at every step of BFS (memorize this shape —
   it's identical in every BFS-on-grid problem):

   while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
       int[] current = queue.poll();   // take the next cell out of the queue
       int r = current[0], c = current[1];

       for (each of the 4 neighbors of (r, c)) {
           if (neighbor is valid AND not visited yet) {
               mark neighbor as visited;
               queue.offer(new int[]{neighborRow, neighborCol});  // add it to the back of the line
           }
       }
   }

   Why is this "ripples spreading outward," like we said with the room example?

   Because of the FIFO nature of the queue: if cell A (distance 0) is processed first,
   its neighbors (distance 1) get added to the back of the queue. Since the queue serves things in the order they were
   added, ALL distance-1 cells get processed before ANY distance-2 cell
   (which only gets added once a distance-1 cell is processed).
   This guarantees BFS explores in perfect "rings" outward — distance 0, then all of distance 1,
   then all of distance 2 — automatically, just from how a queue behaves.

   The "level by level" trick — the one genuinely new idea for grid BFS.

   If you need to know how many steps/minutes something took (not just "is it reachable"),
   you need to track when one "ring" ends and the next begins. This is done by grabbing the queue's current size
   before processing that ring:

   int steps = 0;
   while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
       int size = queue.size();  // exactly how many cells are in THIS ring, right now
       for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
           // process one cell from this ring, add its neighbors for the NEXT ring
       }
       steps++;  // one full ring finished = one step/minute passed
   }


   DFS: recursion, dives deep, visited check + boundary check + base-case check, explores in "as far as possible" order
   BFS: queue, spreads outward in rings, same visited/boundary checks, explores in "distance order,"
   has this extra "ring size" trick when distance/steps matter

